[Music]  the micro environment has always  fascinated me i was not even in grad  school yet  and i heard mina basel give a talk where  she showed  a cell that she had taken from the  prostate and put in a mammary micro  environment  and that cell actually started producing  milk which i thought was the most  fascinating  experiment i'd ever seen  professor ashani ratna has been studying  the cellular micro environment in her  lab for the past  17 years cells don't live in isolation  the other cells molecules and blood  vessels in their surroundings can have a  powerful effect on their behavior  and this is especially important when it  comes to cancer  so the tumor micro environment is really  the neighborhood in which the cells  sit they sit in a network of  matrix and mesh and they  have other cells that are around them  and the impact and the crosstalk  of those cells with the tumor cells can  have a huge  impact on the behavior of the tumor  cells outside of genetics  my lab and others are looking at the  impact of aging  of normal cells in the tumor  microenvironment and how those  impact cancer development and  progression  it's such an obvious thing  that cancer and aging go hand in hand  and yet despite that  until we've really started digging  around i don't think even i realized how  important it would be to  understand what those changes are and  how we can target them  cancer is often defined as a disease of  aging and the vast majority of cases  occur  in people over the age of 50. as global  populations live  longer cancer is set to become a major  public health burden  so how does an aging micro environment  around a tumor  affect cancer progression what we're  finding is that the cells that are aging  normally as they're supposed to do  around the tumor cells are sending  signals to those tumor cells that make  them  more metastatic and more invasive and  more resistant to therapy  for example if you look at the skin we  see collagen  that is nice and firm and very dense and  younger skin  but that we lose that density and that  firmness  in aged skin and that provides ways for  tumor cells to move differentially  through that collagen also immune cells  the immune system changes dramatically  as we age  and what we're finding is that there are  changes that are both beneficial  as well as detrimental in the age tumor  microenvironment  so for example in melanoma patients are  often treated with therapies  that boost the immune system so  immunotherapies and  we find that older patients actually  respond much better  to immunotherapy such as anti-pd1 than  younger patients because they don't have  the same level of regulatory checkpoints  that  younger patients tend to have  so one of the things we've been most  excited about recently is the fact that  we have  found changes that occur in tumor cells  that we put into age microenvironments  rather than young microenvironments  and we can actually target those changes  we see those changes in patients and  when we target them in mice  we can overcome therapy resistance and  reduce metastasis  and so that is fascinating and we're  hoping to push that into clinical trials  over the next few years  so our research is currently paying  enough attention  to the effects of the aging tumor micro  environment  as a community we're not really taking  the aging micro environment into account  we are designing pre-clinical studies  that are all based in  six to eight week old mice we don't  really tailor our therapies from young  to  older patients our work has revealed  that older patients respond very  differently even to targeted therapy so  that has been a really fascinating  finding for us because one of the early  experiments that we did  was to take uh genetically identical  tumors that bore a mutation that we  could target  and yet when we put the same tumors in  either a young mouse or an age mouse  what we saw  was that the tumors in the age mice  despite the fact that you had one gene  and one  drug to target that gene behaved  completely differently  i think one of the most important  milestones we're gonna see over the next  five to ten years is the movement of  some of this work  into the clinic so for example the  target that i was speaking of  earlier is a target that we can make a  drug for  which we are doing and then get into  patients in a clinic  i also hope that people will start  thinking  about designing preclinical studies in  young and aged mice  we're beginning to show that there are  differences in older females and older  males  so i think that's going to be an area of  great importance  as well and finally  really what i'd like to see is the way  we treat patients  how important it is to treat them  according to their  [Music]  age  you

